@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+# Rejection: arXiv 1803.03550
+
+Paper: Marc van Kreveld, Maarten Löffler, and Lionov Wiratma,
+[“On Optimal Polyline Simplification Using the Hausdorff and Fréchet
+Distance”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.03550).
+
+## Decision
+
+No module is shipped. The proposed planted-polyline family fails **H**, on
+both tracks. This is not a failure of exact verification: a subsequence and a
+finite matching/coverage certificate can be checked using exact rational
+arithmetic. It is also not a claim that the paper's worst-case Hausdorff
+problem is easy.
+
+| gate | result | reason |
+|---|---|---|
+| G | potentially passes | a simplification can be planted and its geometric-distance witness carried through a rational similarity |
+| H, Track A | fails | the paper proves worst-case NP-hardness, not hardness for any planted distribution available from its constructions |
+| H, Track B | fails | the constructible shortcuts are either recovered mechanically by the paper's algorithms or exposed by a constant/fixed-block by-hand rule |
+| V | potentially passes | segment distances and a supplied monotone matching can be checked exactly |
+
+## Step 0 findings
+
+Section 2 fixes the native problem: the output is a subsequence of the input
+vertices, including the first and last vertex. Section 4.1 proves that minimum
+undirected-Hausdorff simplification is NP-hard by reducing Hamiltonian cycle in
+segment-intersection graphs. Section 4.2 proves the analogous directed
+`P -> Q` result by reducing minimum line cover. These are worst-case reductions.
+Neither result gives a samplable distribution of certified yes-instances on
+which a domain-standard algorithm fails. Planting a Hamiltonian cycle or a line
+cover would establish G, but the theorem does not establish H for the planted
+distribution; claiming Track A from these theorems would confuse worst-case
+NP-hardness with distributional hardness.
+
+The paper also identifies the easy regimes that rule out an unqualified
+hardness claim:
+
+- Section 4.3 gives a simple `O(n^4)` algorithm for optimal directed
+  Hausdorff simplification in the reverse direction, `Q -> P`: construct the
+  valid-link graph and take a shortest path.
+- The theorem at the end of Section 5 gives an `O(k n^5)` dynamic program and
+  `O(k n^2)` space for optimal Fréchet simplification.
+- Section 1 describes the Imai–Iri valid-link graph; its straightforward
+  implementation is `O(n^3)` and the cited implementations are `O(n^2)` for
+  its local Hausdorff criterion.
+
+This last point kills the prior-triage proposal. If a planted simplification
+has anchors `q_0,...,q_k` and the intermediate input vertices of each block are
+inserted within epsilon of segment `q_i q_{i+1}`, that anchor segment is a
+valid Imai–Iri link. The planted anchor chain is therefore present in the
+shortcut graph by construction, and shortest path returns a simplification no
+longer than the planted one. The certificate is being produced by the
+polynomial shortcut-graph algorithm that the paper itself describes.
+
+## Mechanical cost and compact route
+
+The numbers below are operation-count evaluations of the paper's displayed
+bounds, not timings of an unavailable geometry package.
+
+| candidate regime | shipping-size example | mechanical route | mechanical cost | compact/by-hand route | result |
+|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---|
+| reverse directed Hausdorff | `n=128` | Section 4.3 valid-link construction | at most `128^4 = 268,435,456` primitive-scale steps | none supplied by the paper beyond identifying valid links | no Track-B compression target |
+| Fréchet | `n=32, k=4` | Section 5 dynamic program | `4*32^5 = 134,217,728` transition-scale steps | none for inverse-generated noisy blocks except doing the same link/matching tests | no compact route shorter than the mechanical decision work |
+| Section 3 Hausdorff counterexample | arbitrary `n` | generic optimization | polynomial/exponential depending on direction | fewer than 10 elementary choices: output `p_1`, any even-indexed vertex (so `p_2` works), and `p_n` | fixed-index attack succeeds |
+
+The large gaps in the first two rows do **not** rescue the paper on Track B:
+there is no short structural route supplied for those general instances. The
+one paper construction that does supply such a route is the third row, and it
+is too exposed to qualify. Theorem 1 of Section 3 explicitly says the optimum
+is `⟨p_1,p_i,p_n⟩` for any even `i`; the in-context attack “try
+`⟨p_1,p_2,p_n⟩`” succeeds on every transformed copy because Euclidean
+similarities do not alter subsequence indices. It would score 8/8, whereas a
+Track-B adversary panel requires 0/8 for every by-hand attack.
+
+Randomizing equal block sizes in the noisy planting does not fix this: the
+fixed-block boundary rule reads all anchors directly. Randomizing block sizes
+removes that rule but also removes the promised compact route; exact recovery
+then requires the same shortcut/matching tests used by the mechanical
+algorithm. Thus the compact route is either constant and guessable, or is no
+shorter in kind than the mechanical route.
+
+## Why the NP-hard reduction was not turned into a generator
+
+The Section 4.1 proof says to choose epsilon “sufficiently small” after building
+an arrangement traversal, and obtains a length-`3n+1` simplification exactly
+when the segment-intersection graph has a Hamiltonian cycle. To use this
+honestly on Track A, a generator would still need an independently justified
+distribution of segment representations with known Hamiltonian cycles that
+defeats a Hamiltonian-cycle/branching attack. Planting the cycle is not such a
+justification; random planted Hamiltonian instances are often easier than the
+worst case. No such distributional theorem or construction appears in the
+paper, so the required G5/G6 evidence cannot be inferred from its NP-hardness
+theorem.
+
+Similarly, Section 4.2 uses qualitative “sufficiently far” scaffolding around a
+minimum-line-cover instance. Even after making those inequalities rational and
+exact, inverse-generating an obvious line cover would only move the unanswered
+distributional-hardness question into the source instance.
+
+## What could reopen the decision
+
+This paper could support a future module if one of the following is supplied
+and measured rather than assumed:
+
+1. an exact rational implementation of the Section 4.1 reduction fed by a
+   certified planted segment-intersection distribution on which Hamiltonian
+   branching has 0/8 successes at the shipping size; or
+2. a Track-B polyline construction with a sub-300-operation geometric
+   invariant, while fixed-index, block-boundary, Douglas–Peucker, and simple
+   matching heuristics all fail 0/8.
+
+Neither ingredient is present in the paper or in the prior-triage proposal.
+Building a module now would therefore turn a worst-case theorem into an
+unsupported distributional claim, or ship a family solved by its own reference
+algorithm with no nontrivial compression insight.

