+which a domain-standard algorithm fails. Planting a Hamiltonian cycle or a line
+cover would establish G, but the theorem does not establish H for the planted
+distribution; claiming Track A from these theorems would confuse worst-case
+NP-hardness with distributional hardness.
+
+The paper also identifies the easy regimes that rule out an unqualified
+hardness claim:
+
+- Section 4.3 gives a simple `O(n^4)` algorithm for optimal directed
+  Hausdorff simplification in the reverse direction, `Q -> P`: construct the
+  valid-link graph and take a shortest path.
+- The theorem at the end of Section 5 gives an `O(k n^5)` dynamic program and
+  `O(k n^2)` space for optimal Fréchet simplification.
+- Section 1 describes the Imai–Iri valid-link graph; its straightforward
+  implementation is `O(n^3)` and the cited implementations are `O(n^2)` for
+  its local Hausdorff criterion.
+
+This last point kills the prior-triage proposal. If a planted simplification
+has anchors `q_0,...,q_k` and the intermediate input vertices of each block are
+inserted within epsilon of segment `q_i q_{i+1}`, that anchor segment is a
+valid Imai–Iri link. The planted anchor chain is therefore present in the
+shortcut graph by construction, and shortest path returns a simplification no
+longer than the planted one. The certificate is being produced by the
+polynomial shortcut-graph algorithm that the paper itself describes.
