-Paper: Marc van Kreveld, Maarten Löffler, and Lionov Wiratma,
-[“On Optimal Polyline Simplification Using the Hausdorff and Fréchet
-Distance”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.03550).
+Paper: [On Optimal Polyline Simplification using the Hausdorff and Fréchet Distance](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.03550), Marc van Kreveld, Maarten Löffler, and Lionov Wiratma.
 
 ## Decision
 
-No module is shipped. The proposed planted-polyline family fails **H**, on
-both tracks. This is not a failure of exact verification: a subsequence and a
-finite matching/coverage certificate can be checked using exact rational
-arithmetic. It is also not a claim that the paper's worst-case Hausdorff
-problem is easy.
+No generator is shipped. The native geometric problem clears **V**, and feasibility witnesses can clear **G**, but the available certificate-by-construction families fail **H**. This is an H rejection after checking both tracks, not a rejection because an algorithm merely exists.
 
 | gate | result | reason |
 |---|---|---|
-| G | potentially passes | a simplification can be planted and its geometric-distance witness carried through a rational similarity |
-| H, Track A | fails | the paper proves worst-case NP-hardness, not hardness for any planted distribution available from its constructions |
-| H, Track B | fails | the constructible shortcuts are either recovered mechanically by the paper's algorithms or exposed by a constant/fixed-block by-hand rule |
-| V | potentially passes | segment distances and a supplied monotone matching can be checked exactly |
+| G — generatable | only feasibility, or theorem gadgets with an easy read-off | Subdividing a planted simplification gives a feasible subsequence, but it does not prove that the subsequence is minimum. Theorem 1 gives a known feasible three-vertex simplification, while the hardness reductions need a source-instance witness. |
+| H — Track A | fail | Theorems 4 and 6 are worst-case NP-hardness reductions. They give no average-case or planted-distribution hardness statement. Planting a Hamiltonian cycle or a line cover therefore does not justify hardness of the generated distribution. |
+| H — Track B | fail | For every paper-backed inverse construction found here, the strongest construction-aware certificate extractor is asymptotically the same route as the proposed compact insight; the costs below are comparable. |
+| V — verifiable | pass in principle | A claimed subsequence and its Hausdorff or Fréchet bound can be checked by exact geometric predicates/free-space reachability. This does not establish optimality unless the task is phrased as a bounded decision witness or carries a separately generated lower bound. |
 
-## Step 0 findings
+## Exact definition and easy regimes
 
-Section 2 fixes the native problem: the output is a subsequence of the input
-vertices, including the first and last vertex. Section 4.1 proves that minimum
-undirected-Hausdorff simplification is NP-hard by reducing Hamiltonian cycle in
-segment-intersection graphs. Section 4.2 proves the analogous directed
-`P -> Q` result by reducing minimum line cover. These are worst-case reductions.
-Neither result gives a samplable distribution of certified yes-instances on
-which a domain-standard algorithm fails. Planting a Hamiltonian cycle or a line
-cover would establish G, but the theorem does not establish H for the planted
-distribution; claiming Track A from these theorems would confuse worst-case
-NP-hardness with distributional hardness.
+Section 2 defines a simplification as a subsequence of the input vertices, beginning at `p1` and ending at `pn`, with error at most a supplied `epsilon > 0`. The paper's `OPT_H` uses the directed Hausdorff distance from the input polyline `P` to the simplification `Q` (Section 2). The undirected version is treated separately in Section 4.1.
 
-The paper also identifies the easy regimes that rule out an unqualified
-hardness claim:
+The easy results that rule out careless Track A claims are explicit:
 
-- Section 4.3 gives a simple `O(n^4)` algorithm for optimal directed
-  Hausdorff simplification in the reverse direction, `Q -> P`: construct the
-  valid-link graph and take a shortest path.
-- The theorem at the end of Section 5 gives an `O(k n^5)` dynamic program and
-  `O(k n^2)` space for optimal Fréchet simplification.
-- Section 1 describes the Imai–Iri valid-link graph; its straightforward
-  implementation is `O(n^3)` and the cited implementations are `O(n^2)` for
-  its local Hausdorff criterion.
+- Section 1 gives Imai–Iri's valid-link graph and a shortest-path solution in `O(n^2)` time with the cited implementations.
+- Theorem 7 gives an `O(n^4)` algorithm for the reverse directed Hausdorff problem, from `Q` to `P`.
+- Theorem 10 gives an `O(k n^5)` dynamic program and `O(k n^2)` space for optimal Fréchet simplification.
+- Theorems 1–3 give explicit bad examples for Douglas–Peucker/Imai–Iri, but those examples expose their short optimal simplifications directly; they are counterexamples to heuristics, not hard search distributions.
 
-This last point kills the prior-triage proposal. If a planted simplification
-has anchors `q_0,...,q_k` and the intermediate input vertices of each block are
-inserted within epsilon of segment `q_i q_{i+1}`, that anchor segment is a
-valid Imai–Iri link. The planted anchor chain is therefore present in the
-shortcut graph by construction, and shortest path returns a simplification no
-longer than the planted one. The certificate is being produced by the
-polynomial shortcut-graph algorithm that the paper itself describes.
+The native hard results are Theorem 4 (undirected Hausdorff, via Hamiltonian Cycle in segment-intersection graphs) and Theorem 6 (directed Hausdorff from `P` to `Q`, via Covering Points by Lines). Both are worst-case reductions.
 
-## Mechanical cost and compact route
+## STEP 0 certificate question
 
-The numbers below are operation-count evaluations of the paper's displayed
-bounds, not timings of an unavailable geometry package.
+**What produces the certificate, and what does it cost?**
 
-| candidate regime | shipping-size example | mechanical route | mechanical cost | compact/by-hand route | result |
-|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---|
-| proposed noisy-block plant | `n=128, k=8` | Imai–Iri all-pairs shortcut graph | `C(128,3) = 341,376` intermediate-point/segment checks, plus at most `8,128` graph links | 9 planted block boundaries when blocks are equal | fixed-block attack succeeds |
-| reverse directed Hausdorff | `n=128` | Section 4.3 valid-link construction | at most `128^4 = 268,435,456` primitive-scale steps | none supplied by the paper beyond identifying valid links | no Track-B compression target |
-| Fréchet | `n=32, k=4` | Section 5 dynamic program | `4*32^5 = 134,217,728` transition-scale steps | none for inverse-generated noisy blocks except doing the same link/matching tests | no compact route shorter than the mechanical decision work |
-| Section 3 Hausdorff counterexample | arbitrary `n` | generic optimization | polynomial/exponential depending on direction | fewer than 10 elementary choices: output `p_1`, any even-indexed vertex (so `p_2` works), and `p_n` | fixed-index attack succeeds |
+1. **The proposed noisy-subdivision plant.** Sample a short polyline `Q`, retain its vertices in `P`, and insert intermediate vertices within `epsilon` of its links. The generator knows a feasible `Q`. A construction-aware extractor scans the input once for the planted link changes, so its cost is `Theta(n)` geometric comparisons. The supposed compact route also has to locate those same changes and costs `Theta(n)` comparisons. At the largest size compatible with G9(c), this is at most about **300 operations versus about 300 operations**. There is no compression gap. If the bends are made visibly marked or periodic, both routes drop to `Theta(k)`; if they are hidden well enough to defeat the scan, the generator no longer has a paper-backed compact route for the solver. Moreover, this plant does not certify that `Q` is minimum.
 
-The large mechanical counts do **not** by themselves rescue the paper on Track
-B: there is no short structural route supplied for those general instances.
-The Section 3 construction does supply such a route, but it is too exposed to
-qualify. Its Hausdorff theorem explicitly says the optimum
-is `⟨p_1,p_i,p_n⟩` for any even `i`; the in-context attack “try
-`⟨p_1,p_2,p_n⟩`” succeeds on every transformed copy because Euclidean
-similarities do not alter subsequence indices. It would score 8/8, whereas a
-Track-B adversary panel requires 0/8 for every by-hand attack.
+2. **Theorem 1's three-region construction.** The proof itself gives `Q = <p1, p_i, pn>` for **any even `i`**. A construction-aware algorithm selects `p2` (or another even vertex) using **three index selections and no search**. The compact route is the same **three selections**. Concatenating `g` copies changes both costs to `Theta(g)` and makes the answer longer; it does not open a mechanical-versus-compact gap.
 
-Randomizing equal block sizes in the noisy planting does not fix this: the
-fixed-block boundary rule reads all anchors directly. Randomizing block sizes
-removes that rule but also removes the promised compact route; exact recovery
-then requires the same shortcut/matching tests used by the mechanical
-algorithm. Thus the compact route is either constant and guessable, or is no
-shorter in kind than the mechanical route.
+3. **Theorem 4's reduction.** The certificate is a Hamiltonian cycle of the source segment-intersection graph, carried to a `3n+1`-vertex simplification by Lemma 5. Producing it mechanically requires solving Hamiltonian Cycle in the source instance. Inverse-planting a cycle establishes G but the paper contains no result that planted segment-intersection instances are hard. It therefore cannot support Track A. No polynomial reference algorithm is supplied for Track B.
 
-## Why the NP-hard reduction was not turned into a generator
+4. **Theorem 6's reduction.** The certificate is a minimum line cover of the source point set, carried through the geometric scaffolding. Generating merely a known cover does not certify its minimum size. Generating a specially separated or visibly grouped minimum cover makes the construction-aware extraction and the compact route the same grouping scan, `Theta(n)` versus `Theta(n)`. The paper supplies no hard planted distribution.
 
-The Section 4.1 proof says to choose epsilon “sufficiently small” after building
-an arrangement traversal, and obtains a length-`3n+1` simplification exactly
-when the segment-intersection graph has a Hamiltonian cycle. To use this
-honestly on Track A, a generator would still need an independently justified
-distribution of segment representations with known Hamiltonian cycles that
-defeats a Hamiltonian-cycle/branching attack. Planting the cycle is not such a
-justification; random planted Hamiltonian instances are often easier than the
-worst case. No such distributional theorem or construction appears in the
-paper, so the required G5/G6 evidence cannot be inferred from its NP-hardness
-theorem.
+5. **Theorem 10's Fréchet dynamic program.** On an arbitrary instance its advertised mechanical cost is `O(k n^5)`, which could create a Track B gap. But arbitrary instances do not meet G: the generator would obtain its answer by running that dynamic program. On the proposed planted-subdivision distribution, the planted links are recovered by the same `Theta(n)` construction scan used by the compact route (or by the `O(n^2)` Imai–Iri link graph when local links are used). The relevant strongest method is the construction-aware scan, not the much slower general DP, so the two Track B costs remain comparable.
 
-Similarly, Section 4.2 uses qualitative “sufficiently far” scaffolding around a
-minimum-line-cover instance. Even after making those inequalities rational and
-exact, inverse-generating an obvious line cover would only move the unanswered
-distributional-hardness question into the source instance.
